---
// Task 35: the hero's background is now a single looping video instead of the
// crossfading 4-photo carousel this component originally reproduced (see git
// history / IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md Task 1 for that original RevSlider
// reconstruction) — per explicit request. The `slides`-array/dot-pagination
// system this used to have is removed outright, not left dead alongside the
// video: with a single continuous video there's nothing left to paginate
// between, and /en/ and /de/ never actually diverged from /it/'s slide set
// (Task 9's own comment on the old prop confirmed they were byte-identical),
// so there's no per-language content this loses either.
//
// Task 42: the `poster` attribute (originally the first of the old 4 slide
// photos, shown before the video's own first frame paints) was removed per
// explicit request — a recognizable leftover carousel photo flashing up before
// the video took over read as "the carousel is still there," not as a loading
// state. Replaced with a plain white fallback instead (site.css's own Task 42
// comment on `.hero-slider__video`), which reads as "still loading" rather
// than "a different, older piece of content" — and, paired with Task 42's
// other half (LoadingScreen.astro's reveal now waiting on this video's own
// readiness), should rarely even be visible on a first homepage load anymore.
//

// Task 17: `.hero-fixed-wrapper` reserves real document-flow height equal to the
// viewport minus the header's own rendered height, while `.hero-slider` itself
// becomes `position: fixed`, pinned to start exactly below the header (never
// overlapping it — no z-index reasoning needed for that specific guarantee) and
// fill the rest of the screen. Below 769px, and under prefers-reduced-motion,
// none of that applies — the hero behaves exactly as it did before this task
// (normal block-flow height). See .claude/TODO.md Task 17 for the full design and
// IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md for why this replaces the discarded prior attempt. This
// container-level mechanism is unchanged by Task 35 — it doesn't care whether
// what's filling `.hero-slider` is a photo div or a video, only the box itself.
---

<div class="hero-fixed-wrapper" data-hero-fixed-wrapper>
  <div class="hero-slider">
    <video
      class="hero-slider__video"
      data-hero-video
      muted
      loop
      playsinline
      preload="none"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      <source src="/videos/hero-background.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
    </video>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  // Task 35: autoplay is started from JS, not the `autoplay` HTML attribute,
  // specifically so `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` visitors never even
  // download the video (left at `preload="none"`, poster-only) — matching this
  // project's existing convention of fully disabling motion, not just slowing
  // it down, for that preference (loading screen, Task 17/18's scroll effects).
  // `.play()`'s returned promise is swallowed on rejection: some mobile browsers
  // still refuse autoplay even when muted in certain conditions, and the
  // correct fallback there is simply staying on the poster frame, not an error.
  (function initHeroVideo() {
    const video = document.querySelector<HTMLVideoElement>("[data-hero-video]");
    if (!video) return;
    if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) return;
    video.preload = "auto";
    video.play().catch(() => {});
  })();

  // Task 61: `--header-height` is now needed at every width (mobile's hero
  // height is `calc(100dvh - var(--header-height))`, matching desktop's own
  // `.hero-fixed-wrapper` reservation, per explicit request) — measured here,
  // once at load and on resize, decoupled from the desktop-only fade feature
  // below (which still fully no-ops under prefers-reduced-motion; a plain
  // layout measurement isn't itself a motion effect, so it shouldn't be
  // gated by that preference the way the fade is).
  (function initHeaderHeightVar() {
    const header = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("#site-header");
    if (!header) return;

    function measure() {
      document.documentElement.style.setProperty(
        "--header-height",
        `${header!.getBoundingClientRect().height}px`,
      );
    }

    measure();
    window.addEventListener("resize", measure);
  })();

  // Task 17: toggles `is-hero-faded` on <html> once the user has scrolled past
  // a fixed point just before ".home-philosophy-title" ("Dalla campagna al
  // bicchiere") would enter the viewport from the bottom. See .claude/TODO.md
  // Task 17 for the full design.
  //
  // Deliberately a fixed scroll-position threshold, not an IntersectionObserver on
  // the title itself: the fade must persist for the rest of the page (it must NOT
  // revert once the title scrolls back out of view further down), and is
  // reversible only by scrolling back above the threshold. A plain isIntersecting
  // toggle on the title would flip back to false as soon as the title scrolls
  // above the viewport too, which is wrong — comparing current scrollY against a
  // fixed threshold computed once (and on resize) gets the one-sided-until-
  // scrolled-back-up behavior actually needed. Same pattern already validated in
  // Task 16's bug-fix round (see IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md).
  (function initHeroFullViewport() {
    const header = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("#site-header");
    const titleEl = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".home-philosophy-title");
    if (!header || !titleEl) return;

    if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) return;

    const desktopQuery = window.matchMedia("(min-width: 769px)");
    // The title sits only ~40-70px of real content after the hero's reserved
    // space (confirmed by measuring the actual page — see .claude/TODO.md Task
    // 17), so the buffer must be small and floor-guarded: a larger buffer here is
    // exactly the arithmetic bug that broke Task 16 (it made the threshold go
    // negative, so the fade was active from first paint on every load).
    const TRIGGER_BUFFER_PX = 20;
    const MIN_TRIGGER_SCROLL_PX = 30;

    let active = false;
    let triggerY = 0;

    function docTop(el: HTMLElement) {
      return el.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY;
    }

    function measure() {
      active = desktopQuery.matches;
      if (!active) return;
      triggerY = Math.max(
        MIN_TRIGGER_SCROLL_PX,
        docTop(titleEl!) - window.innerHeight - TRIGGER_BUFFER_PX,
      );
    }

    function update() {
      if (!active) {
        document.documentElement.classList.remove("is-hero-faded");
        return;
      }
      document.documentElement.classList.toggle("is-hero-faded", window.scrollY >= triggerY);
    }

    let ticking = false;
    function onScroll() {
      if (ticking) return;
      ticking = true;
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        update();
        ticking = false;
      });
    }

    function onResize() {
      measure();
      update();
    }

    measure();
    update();
    window.addEventListener("scroll", onScroll, { passive: true });
    window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
    desktopQuery.addEventListener("change", onResize);
  })();

  // Task 18: "brakes" the scroll as the user scrolls down into
  // ".home-philosophy-section" ("Dalla campagna al bicchiere") — the section
  // captures the scroll and animates to a centered rest position before
  // releasing control back to the user, rather than letting them scroll freely
  // past it. See .claude/TODO.md Task 18.
  //
  // This is the standard "scroll-jacking" technique: capture wheel/touch input
  // with preventDefault() while animating window.scrollTo() programmatically,
  // then release the capture once the animated scroll arrives. It replaces two
  // earlier attempts at this same effect: a pure-CSS scroll-snap attempt
  // (removed entirely — see IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md for why root-level
  // scroll-snap turned out to be an unreliable technique in practice, not a
  // tuning problem) and a passive JS version that only nudged the scroll into
  // place *after* it had already come to rest — reported as unsatisfying
  // precisely because it was passive: free scroll, then a corrective jump,
  // rather than an active brake felt during the scroll itself.
  //
  // Only engages once, on the way down, per approach: it doesn't fire scrolling
  // upward (matching "before going over it" — this is specifically about not
  // being able to scroll past on the way down), and the guard resets once the
  // user has scrolled back up near the very top of the page, so scrolling down
  // again later re-triggers it — same reversible convention as every other
  // scroll effect on this page.
  //
  // Triggers as soon as the user starts scrolling down past a small threshold
  // (TRIGGER_SCROLL_THRESHOLD_PX) from the top of the page — not once the
  // section's own position is already nearly in view. Reported live that
  // requiring a long ordinary scroll through the hero before anything happened
  // undercut the "premium" feel this was built for; triggering right as the
  // user leaves the hero makes the whole hero-to-section transition read as one
  // deliberate motion. Because the animation can now cover a much longer
  // distance (from just past the hero all the way to centering the section,
  // rather than a short final approach), its duration scales with that
  // distance — SLIDE_MS_PER_PX added on top of a floor, capped at
  // SLIDE_DURATION_MAX_MS — so a short trigger-to-target distance still feels
  // snappy and a long one doesn't turn into an instant, blurry jump.
  //
  // The slide itself is driven manually frame-by-frame (requestAnimationFrame +
  // duration/easing), not window.scrollTo({behavior:'smooth'}) released on the
  // `scrollend` event — an earlier version did that and was reported as
  // "brakes but doesn't slide, feels groggy". The likely cause: `scrollend`
  // doesn't clearly distinguish the wheel-driven scroll that triggered the
  // brake from the programmatic one meant to animate it, so the two could race
  // and the native smooth-scroll could get interrupted or barely start before
  // release fired. Manually driving every frame removes that ambiguity
  // entirely — the brake has full, deterministic control over when the slide
  // starts, how long it takes, and when it ends.
  //
  // Desktop/tablet only (>=769px): capturing touch input this way is
  // meaningfully more disruptive to touch-scroll physics than to a mouse wheel,
  // and this page's other scroll-driven effects (Task 17) share that same
  // device scope already.
  (function initPhilosophySectionBrake() {
    const header = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("#site-header");
    const section = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".home-philosophy-section");
    if (!header || !section) return;

    if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) return;

    const desktopQuery = window.matchMedia("(min-width: 769px)");
    const TRIGGER_SCROLL_THRESHOLD_PX = 30;
    const SLIDE_DURATION_FLOOR_MS = 400;
    const SLIDE_DURATION_MAX_MS = 1200;
    const SLIDE_MS_PER_PX = 0.3;

    let active = desktopQuery.matches;
    let isBraking = false;
    let hasBraked = false;
    let lastScrollY = window.scrollY;

    function preventScroll(e: Event) {
      e.preventDefault();
    }

    function targetScrollY() {
      const headerHeight = header!.getBoundingClientRect().height;
      const rect = section!.getBoundingClientRect();
      const sectionCenter = rect.top + window.scrollY + rect.height / 2;
      const viewportCenter = headerHeight + (window.innerHeight - headerHeight) / 2;
      return sectionCenter - viewportCenter;
    }

    // Ease-in-out: gentle start (feels like a brake catching, not a jerk),
    // gentle finish (settles into place rather than stopping abruptly).
    function easeInOutQuad(t: number) {
      return t < 0.5 ? 2 * t * t : 1 - (-2 * t + 2) ** 2 / 2;
    }

    function engageBrake() {
      isBraking = true;
      hasBraked = true;
      window.addEventListener("wheel", preventScroll, { passive: false });
      window.addEventListener("touchmove", preventScroll, { passive: false });

      const startY = window.scrollY;
      const endY = targetScrollY();
      const distance = Math.abs(endY - startY);
      const duration = Math.min(SLIDE_DURATION_MAX_MS, SLIDE_DURATION_FLOOR_MS + distance * SLIDE_MS_PER_PX);
      const startTime = performance.now();

      function step(now: number) {
        const progress = Math.min(1, (now - startTime) / duration);
        // Explicit object form + behavior:"auto" (not the 2-arg
        // window.scrollTo(x, y) form) so this stays immune to the site-wide
        // `scroll-behavior: smooth` in site.css — the 2-arg form is subject
        // to that CSS property, and letting the browser layer its own smooth
        // animation on top of a value already being manually eased every
        // frame would fight this animation rather than complement it.
        window.scrollTo({
          top: startY + (endY - startY) * easeInOutQuad(progress),
          left: 0,
          behavior: "auto",
        });
        if (progress < 1) {
          requestAnimationFrame(step);
        } else {
          release();
        }
      }
      requestAnimationFrame(step);

      function release() {
        isBraking = false;
        window.removeEventListener("wheel", preventScroll);
        window.removeEventListener("touchmove", preventScroll);
      }
    }

    function onScroll() {
      const currentY = window.scrollY;
      const scrollingDown = currentY > lastScrollY;
      lastScrollY = currentY;

      if (!active || isBraking) return;

      if (scrollingDown && currentY > TRIGGER_SCROLL_THRESHOLD_PX && !hasBraked) {
        engageBrake();
      } else if (currentY <= TRIGGER_SCROLL_THRESHOLD_PX) {
        hasBraked = false;
      }
    }

    function onResize() {
      active = desktopQuery.matches;
    }

    window.addEventListener("scroll", onScroll, { passive: true });
    window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
    desktopQuery.addEventListener("change", onResize);
  })();

  // Task 21 follow-up: fills the gap between the closing "Un vino che esalta i
  // sensi..." section and the footer. Two earlier attempts (a flat
  // `min-height: 70vh` guess, then a JS-measured min-height on
  // `.grids-s-w_i`) both grew the section's own box and were both reported
  // live as still leaving a gap. Likely cause: `#site-content` (the shared
  // wrapper around the whole page body, every page) has `overflow: hidden`
  // set on it in the theme CSS — growing something deep inside that via
  // min-height doesn't reliably repaint, a different failure class than "the
  // number was too small". See site.css's Task 21 comment for the full
  // reasoning.
  //
  // This measures the same real gap (between the section and #site-footer),
  // but instead of growing the section, it sets a `--closing-gap-fill` custom
  // property on #site-footer itself — #site-footer is a *sibling* of
  // #site-content, not a descendant, so it isn't subject to that clipping.
  // site.css's `#site-footer::before` rule uses that property to extend the
  // footer's own black background upward by exactly the gap amount.
  //
  // Re-measures on resize and via a ResizeObserver on <body> (covers content
  // height changes after the initial run, e.g. late web-font swaps shifting
  // text line height) — reset-then-remeasure on each run converges to a
  // stable value rather than growing indefinitely, since re-applying the same
  // computed value doesn't itself change the observed size again.
  (function initClosingSectionFill() {
    const section = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".home-closing-section .grids-s-w_i");
    const footer = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("#site-footer");
    if (!section || !footer) return;

    function fill() {
      footer!.style.setProperty("--closing-gap-fill", "0px");
      const gap = footer!.getBoundingClientRect().top - section!.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;
      if (gap > 0) {
        footer!.style.setProperty("--closing-gap-fill", `${gap}px`);
      }
    }

    fill();
    window.addEventListener("resize", fill);
    window.addEventListener("load", fill);
    if (typeof ResizeObserver !== "undefined") {
      new ResizeObserver(fill).observe(document.body);
    }
  })();
</script>
